            <ArticleTitle>Value of C-reactive protein in patients with stable angina pectoris, coronary narrowing (30% to 70%), and normal fractional flow reserve.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>702-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This study is the first that combines a serum marker of inflammation (C-reactive protein) and intracoronary-derived fractional flow reserve. A low C-reactive protein level was strongly associated with uncomplicated follow-up in patients with hemodynamic nonsignificant coronary lesions. These results show that C-reactive protein provides additional information relevant for clinical decision-making in patients with intermediate (30% to 70%) coronary lesions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
